# Desktop profile plus a hosted build environment.
#
# raven-postinstall reads exactly one .packages file, so this is a standalone
# list rather than an include of desktop.packages. Every name in
# desktop.packages must therefore also appear here, by hand. The build section
# in the middle is the only part that is this file's alone -- the shared
# content is no longer all of it above that point, so "everything above the
# build section" is not the rule to check against.
#
# This is the check, and it cares about neither ordering nor where a section
# sits, so it survives either file being reorganised:
#
#   cd configs/installer/profiles
#   comm -23 <(grep -v '^#\|^$' desktop.packages | sort) \
#            <(grep -v '^#\|^$' developer.packages | sort)
#
# Anything it prints is a package a desktop install gets and a developer
# install silently does not.
#
# Generating this file from a shared fragment was considered and deliberately
# not done. The only places a generator could run are stage4-iso.sh's
# install_installer -- the function that copies these files into
# /etc/raven/install-profiles -- or a new lazy.toml target, and either way the
# file in the tree stops being the file that ships: reading it would no longer
# tell you what the profile installs, which is the whole reason these are
# plain lists. That is a poor trade for forty duplicated lines that change a
# few times a year, and it would cost the per-profile comments too, since a
# generated file cannot carry a note that is true for one profile and not the
# other. The comm check above is the cheap half of the same guarantee;
# wiring it into `imlazy test` beside check-manifests.sh is the obvious next
# step and is a change to that script rather than to this file.
linux-firmware
sof-firmware
alsa-ucm-conf
pipewire
pipewire-audio
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber
bluez
bluez-utils
mesa-utils
vulkan-tools
vulkan-radeon
vulkan-intel

# The graphics stack huginn links against -- see desktop.packages for why these
# are declared rather than left as the unowned .so files the ISO stages, and
# for why libseat and libudev are not listed by name.
mesa
libdrm
libinput
libxkbcommon
wayland
seatd
xorg-xwayland

base-devel
git
rustup
go

# The development side of what RavenGUI links.
#
# On Arch these are not split -devel packages: headers and .pc files ship in
# the main package, so the graphics names above cover the build too. What is
# missing without this section is the rest of the toolchain's discovery path.
#
# The image ships huginn but carried no header and no .pc file anywhere, so an
# installed system could not rebuild the compositor it was running: `cargo
# check` dies in libseat-sys's build script with "Package libseat was not found
# in the pkg-config search path". A confusing place to land when the binary is
# already on the machine.
#
# pkgconf is what every one of those lookups goes through, and it was otherwise
# present only as a stray unowned file. wayland-protocols is build-only: the
# XML the wayland-scanner build step reads, needed by nothing at runtime.
pkgconf
wayland-protocols

# The toolkit, for RavenFileManager. Kept in step with desktop.packages by
# hand, as everything above the build section is -- raven-postinstall reads
# exactly one .packages file and cannot include another. See desktop.packages
# for why glib2, gsettings-desktop-schemas, shared-mime-info and bubblewrap are
# named when no linker will ever mention them.
gtk4
libadwaita
glib2
gsettings-desktop-schemas
gsettings-system-schemas
shared-mime-info
desktop-file-utils
gtk-update-icon-cache
gdk-pixbuf2
librsvg
glycin
bubblewrap
dconf

# Two names the ISO already carries as unowned copied files.
#
# Same argument as the graphics stack above, applied to two things that have
# nothing to do with the desktop. stage2 copies /usr/bin/nft and
# /usr/bin/sysctl out of the build container, so the image has both binaries
# and `rvn owns` can account for neither: never updated, never repaired.
# Declaring them here puts them under package management on an installed
# machine, which for a firewall in particular is the difference between a
# security fix arriving with the next `rvn update` and not arriving.
#
#   nftables   what the nftables service in /etc/raven/init.toml runs against
#              /etc/nftables.conf. The service ships disabled, so this is the
#              package you want present before you decide to turn the
#              firewall on rather than while you are trying to. Installing it
#              will leave an /etc/nftables.conf.pacnew beside Raven's
#              ruleset: Arch's nftables owns that path too and rvn spares a
#              backup file it did not write, which is the correct outcome
#              here -- the shipped ruleset survives, and the upstream example
#              is there to diff against.
#   procps-ng  `sysctl`, and with it any way to ask whether
#              /usr/lib/sysctl.d/50-raven.conf actually took. ps, top, free
#              and uptime are from the same package and were already on the
#              image by accident of the build container carrying it.
nftables
procps-ng

# Optional in the sense that matters: nothing Raven ships needs any of these,
# and each one is here because software that is not Raven's does.
#
#   polkit   Raven has no polkitd. raven-rc, rvn, raven-powerd and
#            raven-firmware authorise by unix group on their own socket or by
#            already being root, and gain exactly nothing from an
#            authorization framework. What needs one is other people's
#            software: virt-manager, GNOME Disks, graphical updaters and
#            `fwupdmgr` all call CheckAuthorization before they will do
#            anything, and with nobody owning org.freedesktop.PolicyKit1 that
#            call fails rather than prompts. It is not a dialog you can
#            dismiss -- it is the tool not working, with an error that blames
#            D-Bus.
#
#            Installing this creates the polkitd account from the package's
#            own sysusers.d fragment (rvn applies those; systemd is not
#            involved) and activates the polkitd service template, which
#            ships DISABLED. `raven-rc enable polkitd` is the deliberate
#            second step, and /usr/share/raven/services/polkitd.toml is where
#            the whole tradeoff is written down -- including the setuid-root
#            /usr/bin/pkexec this package also brings, which is the honest
#            cost and the reason polkit is not in the base image.
#   flatpak  The answer to application availability. Raven's own repository
#            is small and the AUR path builds from source, unsigned, on the
#            machine you are sitting at -- neither is a way to get a current
#            browser, an IDE or a chat client this afternoon. Flatpak is, and
#            it does it without asking the base system to carry any of those
#            runtimes or their dependency graphs. The portal frontend the
#            sandboxed applications talk to is already shipped, as a user
#            service in configs/raven/user-services/xdg-desktop-portal.toml,
#            so the desktop side of this is in place. Adding a remote is not
#            done here: `flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists flathub ...` is a
#            decision about who may ship software to this machine, and that
#            belongs to the person who owns it.
#   sbctl    Secure Boot key management. RavenBoot is Raven's own EFI binary
#            and is signed by nobody Microsoft has heard of, so a machine
#            with Secure Boot enabled either boots Raven with the vendor keys
#            cleared or does not boot it at all. sbctl is what creates a key
#            set, enrols it while the firmware is in setup mode, and signs
#            \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI and \EFI\raven\raven-boot.efi on the ESP.
#            Far easier to have installed before Secure Boot is turned on
#            than to obtain from a machine that will not boot.
polkit
flatpak
sbctl

# The assembler, linker and object tools: as, ld, ar, strip, objcopy, objdump,
# readelf, nm. Nothing Raven ships calls them, but a good share of what a
# person installs afterwards does. rvn's AUR path builds on this machine, and
# makepkg links with ld and strips every binary it packages; an out-of-tree
# kernel module has to be linked against the running kernel; and readelf and
# objdump are how you answer "why won't this binary load". stage2 copies ldd
# onto the image but none of these, so without this line the first AUR build
# fails on a desktop install.
#
# Declared here and not copied onto the image by copy_system_utils: rvn will
# not install over a file it does not own, so an image-side copy would make
# `rvn install base-devel` refuse on binutils's own files later.
binutils
